<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<!--
  Hardened runtime entitlements. Signing with a Developer ID identity turns the
  hardened runtime on, and the hardened runtime is what makes these necessary:
  without them the app signs and notarizes fine but crashes the moment WKWebView
  tries to JIT — a failure that only ever appears in a signed build, never in
  `tauri dev`.

  Deliberately minimal. Two entitlements people commonly add here are absent:

    com.apple.security.cs.disable-library-validation — not needed. The only
      non-Tauri executable we ship is the floatty-server sidecar, which runs as
      a separate signed process, not a loaded dylib. Adding it would weaken the
      hardened runtime for no gain.

    com.apple.security.cs.allow-dyld-environment-variables — not needed for
      spawning shells through the PTY plugin; child processes get their own
      hardened-runtime context.

  If notarization or launch fails, widen from here one entitlement at a time and
  record why — an entitlement added "just in case" never gets removed.
-->
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
  <!-- WKWebView JIT-compiles JavaScript. -->
  <key>com.apple.security.cs.allow-jit</key>
  <true/>
  <!-- WebKit also maps writable-executable pages outside the JIT entitlement's scope. -->
  <key>com.apple.security.cs.allow-unsigned-executable-memory</key>
  <true/>
</dict>
</plist>
